<!--
  Phase 7 - Stage 4c: termination of security tooling.

  Sensor note: sysmon-modular ships ProcessTerminate as onmatch="include" with
  only user-writable paths listed, so terminations under Program Files were
  never recorded - exactly where security products live. The config now adds
  security-product install paths and process names. System32 was deliberately
  NOT opened: 51 of 67 running processes live there and the volume would be
  noise, not signal.

  Anchor: rule 61607 (0595-win-sysmon_rules.xml, level 0) matches EID 5 on
  windows_eventchannel-decoded events. Group name is "sysmon_event5" - no
  underscore before the digit. Confirmed from the live default rule.

  Design: a single security process ending is already rare here - measured 8
  terminations/hour across ALL monitored paths - so 100370 fires on one. The
  frequency rule above it catches the mass-kill shape (GentleKiller terminates
  400+ processes across 48 vendors), which is a different and worse event.

  Limit: this covers the tooling installed on this host. A product that is not
  present cannot be protected by naming it.
-->
<group name="sysmon,windows,edr_killer,">

  <rule id="100370" level="12">
    <if_sid>61607</if_sid>
    <!-- Long-running service executables only. An earlier version matched the
         Defender install path wholesale and fired on MpCmdRun.exe, which starts
         and exits routinely for scheduled scans and definition updates - a
         guaranteed daily false positive. Transient helper tools ending is
         normal; a resident security SERVICE ending is not. -->
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)[\\]+(MsMpEng|MsSense|SenseIR|NisSrv|MsSecFlt|Sysmon64|Sysmon|wazuh-agent|ossec-agent)\.exe$</field>
    <description>Security tooling terminated: $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.system.computer)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>security_process_terminated,impair_defenses,byovd_chain,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100371" level="14" frequency="4" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>100370</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>win.system.computer</same_field>
    <description>EDR-killer pattern: multiple security processes terminated on $(win.system.computer) within 2 minutes</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
      <id>T1489</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>security_process_terminated,edr_killer,impair_defenses,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 4d - BYOVD correlation. The core of Stage 4.

       A kernel driver load is not suspicious on its own; a security process
       ending is not conclusive on its own. The two together, inside a short
       window, are the shape ESET named as the strongest available signal
       against GentleKiller - and it holds across all 8 variants because it
       keys on SEQUENCE, not on driver identity. A variant nobody has
       catalogued produces the same pattern.

       Mechanics: Wazuh has no general cross-rule correlation. if_matched_sid
       looks BACKWARD from the current event, so this must hang off the LAST
       event in the chain - the kill - and look back for the driver load.
       Ordering matters: written the other way round it would never fire.

       Scoped by hostname so a driver load on one endpoint cannot be paired
       with a kill on another. -->

  <!-- No frequency attribute: Wazuh rejects frequency="1" (must be >1), and we
       want a SINGLE prior match, not a repeated one. if_matched_sid alone gives
       exactly that - it looks back over timeframe for one occurrence. -->
    <!-- Mechanism note (learned the hard way): if_matched_sid does NOT link two
       different events. It re-fires on the SAME events as the referenced rule
       once that rule has matched within the window - it cannot say "event B
       happened after event A".

       What does work: put both halves of the chain in a shared group, then
       count members of that group per host. Two distinct group members inside
       the window means both a driver load and a security-process kill occurred
       on the same machine - which is the BYOVD shape. -->

</group>